# $name

An experiment built on [alhazen](https://github.com/sh4r11f/alhazen).

## Getting started

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"     # no renderer needed for this
pytest                      # the task's own tests, on a fake clock
python run.py --rig configs/rig-sim.yaml --sub s01 --ses 1
```

That last command runs a complete session against the simulated display and
writes a real run directory under `data/` — trials, events and frame timings,
a config snapshot, a session log and a manifest.

`run.py` starts the experiment in any of alhazen's six modes (`--mode demo`,
`movie`, `measure`, `simulate`, `test`, `run`); each ships with a rig config
built for it, below.

## What is where

| | |
|---|---|
| `src/$package/task.py` | the experiment: parameters, events, outcomes, and what a trial is made of |
| `configs/task.yaml` | the task's parameters for a session |
| `configs/rig-sim.yaml` | run headless: no window, no devices — tests, CI, ssh |
| `configs/rig-view.yaml` | look at the stimulus (`--mode demo`) on this machine's own panel |
| `configs/rig-auto.yaml` | watch the task run itself (`--mode simulate`), dashboard up |
| `configs/rig-mouse.yaml` | play the task yourself (`--mode test`), mouse cursor as gaze |
| `configs/rig-mac.yaml` | any dev mode on a Mac — read its Retina notes before judging a stimulus |
| `configs/rig-lab.yaml` | the rig: fill in your monitor's real geometry and uncomment the devices it has |
| `tests/` | the task's tests, using `alhazen.testing`'s doubles |

Every rig file that opens a window carries starting-point monitor numbers,
not measurements. The numbers decide what a degree of visual angle is, so
measure them for each machine (`alhazen calibrate ruler` checks them with a
tape measure) — that is why there is one rig file per machine and purpose
rather than one shared one.

## On the rig

```bash
alhazen check-rig --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --pulse   # before the subject arrives
alhazen run --task $task_name --rig configs/rig-lab.yaml --sub s01 --ses 1
```

`check-rig` constructs the same device backends a session would, so a clean
check predicts a working session. `--pulse` fires one reward pulse and one
pulse per sync line, so you can hear the pump and see the lines.

The lab config enables the live browser dashboard. It updates after each
trial but remains read-only while the task is running. Press `P` on the
experimenter keyboard before using Resume, Calibrate, Give reward, Quit, or
training-stage controls in the browser; this prevents browser focus from
interfering with an active subject trial.

## Afterwards

```bash
alhazen report --run data/sub-s01/ses-001/run-01_task-$task_name
```

Trial counts by outcome, frame-drop statistics, and a manifest check. With
`--neural <recording dir>` it also aligns the session's clock to the
recording's and measures the display's latency from the photodiode.
